Aim
To create basic dashboards in Tableau by combining multiple visualizations.

Description
A dashboard is a collection of multiple visualizations presented in a single screen. Dashboards help
users compare different aspects of a dataset and understand overall performance quickly.

Uses
• Combining multiple charts in one view.
• Monitoring key performance indicators.
• Comparing categories and regions.
• Supporting interactive analysis.
• Presenting information to decision makers.

Algorithm
1. Open Tableau Public.
2. Connect to the dataset.
3. Create multiple worksheets.
4. Open the Dashboard tab.
5. Drag sheets into the dashboard.
6. Add filters and interactive elements.
7. Arrange the layout properly.
8. Save the dashboard.

Program
1 import pandas as pd
2 import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
3
4 # Load dataset
5 data = pd.read_csv("sales_data.csv")
6
7 # Bar chart
8 data.groupby("Category")["Sales"].sum().plot(kind="bar")
9 plt.title("Sales by Category")
10 plt.show()
11
12 # Pie chart
13 data.groupby("Region")["Sales"].sum().plot(
14 kind="pie", autopct="%1.1f%%")
15 plt.title("Sales by Region")
plt.show()
17
18 # Line chart
19 plt.plot(data["Quantity"], data["Sales"])
20 plt.title("Sales vs Quantity")
21 plt.xlabel("Quantity")
22 plt.ylabel("Sales")
23 plt.show()

Program Explanation
• The dataset is loaded using Pandas.
• A bar chart shows sales by category.
• A pie chart shows the regional contribution to sales.
• A line chart shows the relationship between quantity and sales.
• In Tableau, these individual visualizations can be placed together in one dashboard.

Libraries / Functions Used
• Pandas: Used for data loading and grouping.
• Matplotlib: Used for creating the sample charts.
• Tableau: Used to combine visualizations into an interactive dashboard.

Sample Output

The program produces three analytical views which can be combined in Tableau into a single inter-
active dashboard.

Result
